Architecture heritage
---------------------
The overall architecture — event-based parser, Rowan CST, Salsa
incremental database, HIR and IDE layering — follows the design of
rust-analyzer, which is distributed under MIT OR Apache-2.0.
Upstream: https://github.com/rust-analyzer/rust-analyzer

The source used to say where in particular. Those notes were removed in
March 2026 together with the annotations naming copyleft sources, though
nothing was wrong with them, so the attribution is restated here crate
by crate:

  * crates/syntax — the architecture of rust-analyzer's syntax crate.
  * crates/base-db — source files kept in a concurrent map outside the
    Salsa database, as rust-analyzer does.
  * crates/stdx — the threading module: spawning threads by intent
    (quality of service) and the worker pool follow rust-analyzer's
    stdx thread module, whose documentation was carried over with it.
  * crates/paths — the absolute and relative path wrapper types.
  * crates/cfg — graph vertices that refer to HIR body ids rather than
    to syntax, following rust-analyzer's HIR patterns. This is the
    rust-analyzer share only; the separate notice on crates/cfg below
    is not answered by it.
  * crates/dataflow — rust-analyzer's MIR dataflow for constant
    evaluation and its type-inference lattice, named as references.
  * crates/hir-def — documentation as a first-class part of the HIR,
    the owner of a body (rust-analyzer's DefWithBodyId), and
    segment-by-segment path resolution.
  * crates/hir-ty — type inference in a crate of its own, with
    diagnostics collected as a by-product of inference.
  * crates/hir — a single representation of definitions.
  * crates/ide-db — LineIndex cached through Salsa.

Each entry covers what the removed note named and no more of the crate.

Language heritage
-----------------
BSL and the SDBL query language are part of the 1C:Enterprise platform.
The language itself, its keywords, its syntax and its semantic rules
belong to 1C as the platform author; they are not proprietary to any
third-party reimplementation.

Official references:
  * 1C query language: https://its.1c.ru/db/pubqlang
  * 1C code standards: https://its.1c.ru/db/v8std

Platform help
-------------
The analyzer binary carries crates/bsl-platform/data/platform_facts.json:
the interface facts of the 1C:Enterprise platform help — type, method,
function, constructor and property names, parameter lists, types,
versions, execution contexts and signature lines — and no descriptive
text. It contains none of 1C's descriptions, parameter documentation,
examples or notes; a test fails if any such field appears. The full help
corpus, whose texts are the copyright of ООО «1С-Софт», never enters this
repository or the binary: it is read at run time from the user's own
platform installation or from a help package,
each carrying its own NOTICE.md. See crates/bsl-platform/data/PROVENANCE.md.

Diagnostic rules
----------------
Code-quality diagnostics in crates/ide-diagnostics encode the 1C
code-style standards published at v8std.ru (CC0) and in the official
1C documentation. They do not implement a third party's proprietary
rule set.

