Notice for crates written with a copyleft reference open
---------------------------------------------------------
The notice above covers the grammar layers only. Three crates that were
licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0 when this notice was written, and are MIT now
(see LICENSING.md), were also written with a
copyleft-licensed project open as the working reference. The three are
not equivalent: the first two inherited structure, the third matched
observable behaviour on purpose and copied nothing. In each case
the annotations saying so were removed from the source in March and
April 2026 without the code being rewritten, so the source as it stands
today carries no trace of this. It is therefore stated here.

  * crates/cfg — the control-flow-graph model was written with the CFG
    package of bsl-language-server (LGPL-3.0-or-later) open, by way of
    an earlier Rust project by the same author. The file headers read
    "Ported from BSL Language Server (Java)" and carried per-method
    "Maps to ... in Java" annotations. The builder architecture is
    independent: a recursive descent over HIR, where the reference uses
    a block writer over a parse tree, and the two share no structural
    device. What follows the reference is the vertex and edge taxonomy,
    its granularity, the decomposition of loops, try/except and labels,
    and the part of the graph API that is not the underlying graph
    library's own vocabulary. That identified taxonomy and API slice has
    now been independently re-derived from Chapter 4 of the 1C Developer's
    Guide and control-flow literature, then verified against the previous
    analysis results. This closes that specific slice,
    not the workspace's MIT-only programme or its dependency restrictions.

  * crates/bsl-metadata — the crate described itself as "a Rust port of
    the Java mdclasses library" (https://github.com/1c-syntax/mdclasses,
    LGPL-3.0) when it was created, with per-method "Java equivalent"
    annotations. The parsing implementation is independent, and most of
    the model is dictated by the 1C configuration export format itself,
    which is the format this crate has to read. What is inherited is the
    ontology: the set of named types, the split into traits, the builder
    pattern, and part of the method naming. That identified slice has
    now been re-derived from 1C Designer XML artifacts (three
    configurations and eight extensions, 97 996 documents) and from the
    analyzer's own needs: every decision of the slice is linked to an
    observed artifact or a consumer, the never-populated support-variant
    stub and six public methods with neither source nor caller were
    removed, and the rest kept its form where the derivation reached it.
    This closes that slice,
    not the fixtures listed below or the MIT-only programme.

  * crates/hir-def — several diagnostic ranges and metrics were written
    to reproduce the observable output of bsl-language-server and of the
    ANTLR-generated parser it uses. Those commits changed comments only;
    no code was copied. The behaviour was kept deliberately, so that the
    two tools report the same numbers on the same source.

The identified crates/cfg and crates/bsl-metadata slices are closed as
recorded above. Per-crate licenses and the restrictions arising from
dependencies are unchanged.

